# display-demo

A portable application whose output is a picture rather than an exit code. It
draws three RGB565 colour bars, holds them, and rotates them twice, so that a
person can tell a working panel from a still one.

It names mm.display for the frame and mm.mcu for the delay, and no board, SDK,
or vendor. mm.mcu is not decoration: a provider that owns the display releases
it when the process ends, so an application that draws and returns shows
nothing. Holding the frame is what makes this visible, and holding requires a
clock.

The bars are the three colours whose RGB565 encoding is worth checking by eye.
Red is 0xf800, which is the bytes 0xf8 and 0x00 in that order; a provider that
swaps them draws blue instead, which is exactly the mistake this catches
without a test harness.
